Why a specific grant and a bounded generic grant, not one open-ended
grant
--------------------------------------------------------------------
Paragraph 1 names Intel MKL specifically, the way the GCC Runtime
Library Exception, the GNU Classpath linking exception, the classic
"OpenSSL exception" used by many GPL projects, and Leela Chess Zero's
NVIDIA CUDA/cuDNN exception each name the specific non-free component
they permit (see "Precedents" below). A named grant is the safer,
better-precedented, more defensible form for a copyright holder to
issue: it is unambiguous about what was agreed to, it is easy for a
contributor to understand what they are consenting to, and it does not
inadvertently authorise linking with software nobody has reviewed.
Paragraph 2 adds a narrow, functionally-bounded fallback -- restricted
to a drop-in replacement for MKL in the exact BLAS/LAPACK/PARDISO role
`pypardiso` already fills -- so that switching accelerator vendors (for
example to AMD AOCL or another vendor's PARDISO-compatible solver) does
not require re-drafting and re-clearing this exception with every
contributor each time. It is deliberately not a general "any non-free
library" grant.

Precedents (verified 2026-09-30; not invented)
-----------------------------------------------
- FSF-suggested "Additional permission under GNU GPL version 3 section 7"
  template (the wording paragraph 1 above is adapted from), reproduced
  by SPDX as the "GPL-3.0 Linking Exception (with Corresponding
  Source)": https://spdx.org/licenses/GPL-3.0-linking-source-exception.html
  (short form without the Corresponding Source sentence:
  https://spdx.org/licenses/GPL-3.0-linking-exception.html). Origin: the
  Free Software Foundation's GPL FAQ, https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-faq.html
  (this session could not re-fetch that page live because of a
  transient rate limit; the text is cross-verified via SPDX and via its
  real-world use below, but counsel should pull the FAQ page directly).
- Leela Chess Zero (GPLv3 chess engine) added exactly this template,
  naming "NVIDIA Corporation's libraries from the NVIDIA CUDA Toolkit
  and the NVIDIA CUDA Deep Neural Network library", to let it distribute
  Windows binaries with the proprietary CUDA/cuDNN DLLs. Live in the
  file header of every relevant source file, e.g.
  https://github.com/LeelaChessZero/lc0/blob/master/src/main.cc . The
  maintainers first collected an explicit "I agree" from every
  contributor of copyrightable code before adding it:
  https://github.com/LeelaChessZero/lc0/issues/184 (see "Contributor
  consent" below). This is the closest verified real-world precedent
  for a proprietary vendor math/compute library linked into a copyleft
  scientific-computing project.
- The "OpenSSL exception", used by many GPLv2-era projects to permit
  linking with the OpenSSL library (a license the FSF considers
  GPL-incompatible): "In addition, as a special exception, the
  copyright holders give permission to link the code of portions of
  this program with the OpenSSL library ... and distribute linked
  combinations including the two." Text as catalogued by ScanCode:
  https://scancode-licensedb.aboutcode.org/openssl-exception-gpl-2.0.html .
  This is the oldest and most widely reused precedent for "a free
  program + one named non-free library", though it predates GPLv3
  section 7 and uses GPLv2-era "special exception" phrasing rather than
  the section 7 form; for an AGPLv3-or-later project the section 7 form
  used above is the closer fit.
- GCC Runtime Library Exception (a section 7 style additional permission
  the FSF itself issues for GCC's runtime libraries, to let them be
  linked into proprietary compiled programs):
  https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gcc-exception-3.1.en.html . Cited here
  only as a well-known example of the FSF using the same section-7
  mechanism for a similar purpose (letting a copylefted component be
  linked into code under other terms), not as a source of wording --
  its exact drafting solves a different problem (compiler runtime
  headers, not a proprietary math library) and this session did not
  re-fetch its current text live.
- GNU Classpath linking exception (lets GPL'd Java class libraries be
  linked with independently-licensed application code):
  https://www.gnu.org/software/classpath/license.html . Cited for the
  same reason as the GCC exception above -- a known, FSF-sanctioned
  section-7-style precedent for the general mechanism, not a source of
  wording for this grant; this session did not re-fetch its current
  text live either.

